","IC50_Dark_value":0.08,"Method":"Crystal violet","DOI":"10.1002/chem.201805626","Year":2019,"Type":"IC50","comparison":{"compound_id":"OBMD011700","measurement_id":"MCTM-036101","value":"13.6 ± 0.9","numeric":13.6}}],"logp":[{"abbreviation_in_the_article":"III","value":0.97,"value_relation":"=","std":0.09,"type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"200 mM NaCl","temperature_C":25,"doi":"10.1002/chem.201805626","year":2019,"record_id":"MLDB-002154","detection":"FAAS","method_description":"The partition coefficients (P) of the investigated platinum complexes were determined by the “shake flask” method. The complexes were dissolved in octanol-saturated water (OSW) containing 200 mm NaCl and mixed with water-saturated octanol (WSO) in volumetric ratios of 2:1, 1:1, and 1:2. Mixing was done by vortexing for 30 min at room temperature to establish the partition equilibrium. To separate the phases, centrifugation was done at 3000 g for 5 min. The layers were separated carefully by using a fine-tip pipette and then analyzed for metal content by flameless atomic absorption spectroscopy (FAAS). The partition coefficients were calculated as the ratio of the concentration of the compound in the octanol layer to that in the aqueous layer, log P=log ([Pt]WSO/[Pt]OSW)."}]}